window.SN_DOCS_INDEX=[{"u":"agents.html","t":"Agents, teams & voice","g":"Station & agents","k":"Reference","d":"","h":[{"id":"recruiting","t":"Recruiting"},{"id":"classes-and-archetypes","t":"Classes and archetypes"},{"id":"the-dossier-learns-you","t":"The dossier learns you"},{"id":"the-model-is-a-roster-property","t":"The model is a roster property"},{"id":"concurrency","t":"Concurrency"},{"id":"delegation-agents-that-summon-agents","t":"Delegation: agents that summon agents"},{"id":"live-voice-talking-to-the-station","t":"Live Voice — talking to the station"},{"id":"comms","t":"COMMS"}],"s":[{"id":"recruiting","t":"Recruiting","b":"New agents come through the Recruitment Bay. Each recruit has a dossier — class, loadout, personality, and the skills it ships with. A short interview at creation shapes what the agent knows about you and your goals; the dossier keeps growing as you work together."},{"id":"classes-and-archetypes","t":"Classes and archetypes","b":"StarNet ships a roster of built-in classes (researcher, builder, marketer, analyst, and more), each with a loadout tuned to its job. None are gated — every archetype is available from the start, and every agent draws on the station's shared gear."},{"id":"the-dossier-learns-you","t":"The dossier learns you","b":"The dossier isn't static config — it's a living study of its commander. Agents notice patterns in how you work and what you approve, and propose beliefs (\"prefers a working demo over a written spec\"); each proposal is yours to accept or strike, and the full ledger of what your crew believes about you is readable and editable at any time. Ratings you give on finished work feed a taste profile, so delivery style converges on what you actually value. Nothing is learned behind your back: proposals are explicit, and struck beliefs stay struck."},{"id":"the-model-is-a-roster-property","t":"The model is a roster property","b":"Each agent is pinned to a model on the roster — not per-chat. Put your heavy reasoning model on the architect and a fast cheap model on the intern, and route work accordingly. Swap an agent's model at any time in its dossier."},{"id":"concurrency","t":"Concurrency","b":"Each additional bay or specialist you place can run concurrently — genuinely distinct, bounded agent runs, not one queue wearing costumes. The conveyor carries real work items between rooms."},{"id":"delegation-agents-that-summon-agents","t":"Delegation: agents that summon agents","b":"An agent holding the task delegation capability can break a large job apart and hand the pieces out. Every worker it creates runs its own real agent loop — its own searching, reading, file writing and memory — and returns a result the lead folds back into the answer. There are three shapes: Move What it does Dispatch Hand subtasks to specialists already on your crew. Spawn Create ephemeral clones of itself to work subtasks in parallel, then discard them. Summon Recruit a genuinely new specialist onto the crew — the same thing you'd do in the Recruitment Bay, done live because the job needed it. Background workers are inspectable while they run: list them, look at one, interrupt one that's going the wrong way, or resume a stalled one — it restarts on the same prompt and appends to the same durable record, so nothing is silently lost. You watch the whole tree work on the station floor; a lit desk is a live worker, not a decoration."},{"id":"live-voice-talking-to-the-station","t":"Live Voice — talking to the station","b":"You can put COMMS down and just talk. Hands-free Live Voice listens, transcribes, runs the work, and speaks the answer back, using a speech engine built into the app — Whisper for listening, Kokoro for speaking, both on your CPU. It deliberately does not use a vendor's realtime voice API, even where one is available. A vendor path gave smoother turns but tied the feature to one provider, capped the persona behind that vendor's guardrails, and made the station behave differently depending on which provider you happened to connect. The built-in engine is a little slower per turn and wins everything else: no key, no account, identical on every station, and your recorded audio never leaves the machine. The first session may download the open model weights once. After that, listening is offline; speaking is offline while Kokoro is available and otherwise may use the keyless Microsoft Edge Read Aloud fallback for answer text."},{"id":"comms","t":"COMMS","b":"The COMMS window is command: talk to any agent, stream its work live, approve consequential actions, and answer the (rare, bounded) clarifying question before a big task commits. When an agent needs a decision it asks once with concrete choices — it can't ask and quietly mutate things at the same time; the harness blocks write tools until the question is settled."}]},{"u":"autonomy.html","t":"Autonomy controls","g":"Repeat & automate","k":"Reference","d":"","h":[{"id":"the-autonomy-model","t":"The autonomy model"},{"id":"night-shift","t":"Night Shift"},{"id":"stopping-work","t":"Stopping work"},{"id":"honest-reporting","t":"Honest reporting"},{"id":"schedules","t":"Schedules"},{"id":"quests","t":"Quests"}],"s":[{"id":"the-autonomy-model","t":"The autonomy model","b":"Autonomy in StarNet is a dial, not a switch: you set initiative (how proactive agents may be) and reach (what they may touch) explicitly. Nothing self-escalates. The honest pitch: StarNet is not an \"autopilot for your life\" — you direct; it executes and proposes."},{"id":"night-shift","t":"Night Shift","b":"When you step away, Night Shift takes over: agents pick up standing goals, continue queued work, and explore ideas — inside the leash you configured. Every away-run is jailed : real deliverables are produced in a sandboxed workshop area, and touching anything outside it requires a grant you gave beforehand."},{"id":"stopping-work","t":"Stopping work","b":"Use Stop in COMMS to interrupt the current run. Pause routines or loops in their own panels, and set Night Shift initiative to WAIT to prevent new away-work. If an older save has automation paused by a previous global stop, use RESUME AUTOMATION to clear that saved pause. Recovery preserves your existing permissions and job settings."},{"id":"honest-reporting","t":"Honest reporting","b":"When you come back you get a digest of what actually happened: which runs executed, what they produced, what they cost, and what was declined or blocked. Deliverables open with one click. Work that landed while you were watching is shown as it lands — StarNet distinguishes \"shown to you\" from \"done while nobody watched\" and never blurs the two."},{"id":"schedules","t":"Schedules","b":"Cron-style routines run real agent sessions on a timer — a morning brief, a nightly digest, a weekly cleanup. Every scheduled run surfaces as a readable session afterwards: same transcripts, same ledger, same honesty as attended work. Scheduled runs can notify you through your connected channels when they finish."},{"id":"quests","t":"Quests","b":"The quest system turns standing goals into visible, refreshing work the station proposes and tracks. Quests are generated from your actual dossier and interests — and like everything else, a quest card only ever reflects real backing state."}]},{"u":"connect-a-platform.html","t":"Connect a service","g":"Connect your tools","k":"Guide","d":"","h":[{"id":"which-route-do-i-need","t":"Which route do I need?"},{"id":"route-1-a-remote-mcp-server","t":"Route 1 — a remote MCP server"},{"id":"route-2-an-api-key-web-request","t":"Route 2 — an API key + web_request"},{"id":"paste-the-key","t":"Paste the key"},{"id":"give-the-agent-a-dish","t":"Give the agent a dish"},{"id":"ask-for-the-thing-you-want","t":"Ask for the thing you want"},{"id":"your-key-is-never-shown-to-the-model","t":"Your key is never shown to the model"},{"id":"uploading-files-mockups-images-print-files","t":"Uploading files — mockups, images, print files"},{"id":"letting-it-run-while-you-are-away","t":"Letting it run while you are away"},{"id":"when-it-does-not-work","t":"When it does not work"},{"id":"what-starnet-will-not-do","t":"What StarNet will not do"}],"s":[{"id":"which-route-do-i-need","t":"Which route do I need?","b":"There are three, and the right one depends on what the platform publishes. Check in this order: Does the platform publish a remote MCP server? Then add it in TOOLSETS & CONNECTORS MCP CONNECTORS , or find it already sitting in CATALOG . You get ready-made tools, and it is the least work. Notion, Linear, Stripe, GitHub and Sentry are here. Does it publish only a local stdio MCP server? Put an agent on the SAFE CELL execution profile, choose STDIO (Safe Cell) in MCP CONNECTORS, and enter the command and exact arguments. StarNet runs it inside that agent's persistent Docker environment, never as an interactive host child. Otherwise it has a plain REST API. Paste its key in KEYS and your agent calls the API directly with web_request . This is the route for most of the long tail — including every print-on-demand and marketplace service we checked. Print-on-demand and marketplace platforms are route 2 today. As of July 2026 there is no remote MCP server for Printify, Printful, Etsy or WooCommerce — the only Printify MCP that exists is a local npx server. StarNet can run that server in a Safe Cell; the REST-key route remains the lighter option when you do not want to maintain a container command."},{"id":"route-1-a-remote-mcp-server","t":"Route 1 — a remote MCP server","b":"Open TOOLSETS & CONNECTORS (dock: &#8644; TOOLSETS ). CATALOG — browse vetted servers. &#9656; no setup connects instantly, API key takes a pasted token, OAUTH opens a browser sign-in. One click and its tools are live. MCP CONNECTORS — for anything not in the catalog. Give it a short id, paste the server's Streamable-HTTP endpoint (usually ending /mcp ), and a bearer token if it needs one. A local stdio command ( npx … , uvx … ) requires a named agent on the SAFE CELL profile and a working Docker runtime. Its command uses exact argv with no shell. If Docker is unavailable, the connector stays honestly disconnected; StarNet never falls back to spawning it on the interactive host."},{"id":"route-2-an-api-key-web-request","t":"Route 2 — an API key + web_request","b":""},{"id":"paste-the-key","t":"Paste the key","b":"TOOLSETS & CONNECTORS KEYS PICK A PLATFORM . Choose your platform (or type any name), paste your key, and save. StarNet stores it locally and shows only the last four characters, ever. Each key becomes a named environment variable — Printify becomes PRINTIFY_API_KEY . Adding the platform's API docs URL is worth the ten seconds: it rides into the agent's prompt, so it can look up the right endpoint instead of guessing."},{"id":"give-the-agent-a-dish","t":"Give the agent a dish","b":"A key on its own does nothing. Tools come from placed props : the dish grants the web toolset, which is what contains web_request . Place one in the agent's bay from the build menu. (If you want the agent to use the key from a terminal instead, that is the workbench prop — but for calling an API you do not need it.)"},{"id":"ask-for-the-thing-you-want","t":"Ask for the thing you want","b":"List my Printify shops and tell me which products are unpublished. The agent calls the API, you approve the call, and it reports back."},{"id":"your-key-is-never-shown-to-the-model","t":"Your key is never shown to the model","b":"This is the part worth understanding, because it is unusual. The agent never receives your key. It writes a placeholder naming the key, and StarNet substitutes the real value at the moment the request leaves your machine: web_request url: https://api.printify.com/v1/shops.json headers: { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer ${PRINTIFY_API_KEY}\" } The model sees only the name. A leaked transcript, a log, or a model provider never sees the value, and the response comes back with any echoed secret scrubbed. Placeholders work in headers only — StarNet refuses to send a URL the model wrote containing a secret, because URLs leak into browser history, proxy logs and Referer headers. Some APIs will not take a header and want the key as a query parameter instead. That still works, and the rule is unchanged: the agent names the key and the slot, and StarNet attaches the value itself , after the agent has finished composing the request. web_request url: https://api.example.com/v1/items auth: { \"key\": \"SOME_API_KEY\", \"in\": \"query\", \"name\": \"api_key\" } The agent still never handles the value, and it is attached only to the first request — if the server redirects somewhere else, the credential is not carried along."},{"id":"uploading-files-mockups-images-print-files","t":"Uploading files — mockups, images, print files","b":"Upload endpoints take file bytes , and the same substitution idea covers them. The agent never pastes file contents into a request; it writes a reference to a file in its workspace, and StarNet reads and encodes the bytes at the moment the request is sent. Printify's image upload, for example: web_request url: https://api.printify.com/v1/uploads/images.json method: POST headers: { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer ${PRINTIFY_API_KEY}\" } body: {\"file_name\":\"mockup.png\",\"contents\":\"${file:mockups/mockup.png}\"} For APIs that want a classic form upload ( multipart/form-data ) there is a multipart parameter that attaches workspace files as form fields the same way. Either route: the file must live in the agent's own workspace (it can never reach outside it), the bytes never pass through the model, and the limit is 20MB per file / 25MB per request."},{"id":"letting-it-run-while-you-are-away","t":"Letting it run while you are away","b":"By default a key is usable only in watched sessions — you are at the station and approve the call. Scheduled routines, Night Shift, and agents messaged over Telegram cannot spend it. To change that for one platform, tick usable in scheduled & messaged runs on that key's row in KEYS. It is per key, off by default, and revocable at any time — the next tool call sees the change immediately. Nothing else about unattended runs widens: they still cannot run shell commands, and they still cannot spend any key you have not ticked."},{"id":"when-it-does-not-work","t":"When it does not work","b":"What you see What it means no enabled service key provides X_API_KEY The placeholder name does not match a key you saved, or that key is switched off. Check the exact env-var name shown on the key's row in KEYS. this run is unattended and X’ is not approved for unattended use A scheduled or messaged run tried to spend a watched-only key. Tick the unattended box on that key, or run the task while you are at the station. The agent has no web_request No dish is placed in that agent's bay, or the web toolset is switched off in TOOLSETS. HTTP 401 / 403 from the platform The key reached the platform and the platform rejected it — wrong key, wrong scope, or the wrong header name. Check the platform's docs for the header it expects. A connector sits red on stdio The selected owner is no longer on SAFE CELL , Docker is unavailable, or the command failed inside the container. Restore that profile/runtime and reload; StarNet will not run it on the host. refusing private/loopback/intranet host Working as intended: agents cannot reach your local network or cloud metadata endpoints. the page did not finish loading within 30s (browser) The site stalled the agent's browser — often a slow origin or a bot challenge. The load is cancelled so the browser stays usable; try browser.get_text to see what did load. For a platform you have a key for, its API via web_request is the more reliable route than reading its web pages. A public page returns 403 to the agent Some sites (Etsy among them) refuse automated browsers and direct fetches outright. StarNet does not disguise its requests to get around that. Use the platform's API instead."},{"id":"what-starnet-will-not-do","t":"What StarNet will not do","b":"Show your key to the model, write it to a file, or send it anywhere except the platform you named. Forward your credential across a redirect to a different host. Spawn a local MCP child directly in your interactive host session; stdio is Safe-Cell-only. Spend a key in an unattended run you did not explicitly approve for that."}]},{"u":"connectors.html","t":"Connectors & MCP reference","g":"Connect your tools","k":"Reference","d":"StarNet built-in capabilities, MCP connectors, isolated local stdio servers, messaging channels, and harness interoperability.","h":[{"id":"built-in-tools","t":"Built-in tools"},{"id":"browsing-that-actually-browses","t":"Browsing that actually browses"},{"id":"mcp-connectors","t":"MCP connectors"},{"id":"messaging-channels","t":"Messaging channels"},{"id":"telegram-as-a-remote-control","t":"Telegram as a remote control"},{"id":"harness-interop","t":"Harness interop"},{"id":"local-by-default","t":"Local by default"}],"s":[{"id":"built-in-tools","t":"Built-in tools","b":"Agents ship with a broad real-tool belt. Everything an agent can reach outside its own reasoning belongs to one of eight capability families , and every family is gated by the station's capability model ( object = capability ) — you grant it by placing the gear, and you can revoke it the same way. Family What it grants Web & browser Search the web, fetch pages, and drive a real browser — navigate, read, click, type. File cabinet Read, search, and write files in the agent's workspace. Workbench Run shell commands and verify code — the code-execution bench. Task delegation Delegate subtasks to summoned crew, summon new agents, and schedule routines. Comms relay Send a message out to a connected chat — only chats someone already opened with this station. Media studio Generate images from text and analyse images, on the key the agent already uses. Memory notebook The agent's private memory, task plans, and saved skills. Jukebox Search and control your Spotify — play, pause, queue, \"what's playing\"."},{"id":"browsing-that-actually-browses","t":"Browsing that actually browses","b":"The web family is not just a search box. An agent can run a search, fetch a page, and then drive a real browser against it — navigating, reading the rendered page, clicking, and typing. When a page is unreachable or throws up a challenge, the agent reports that as the result rather than inventing a plausible summary of a page it never loaded; a 403 or a 404 is an answer, not a hidden failure. When a site genuinely requires a login, the agent can't and won't ask you for the password. It asks the station to open a visible browser window and you sign in yourself — your password is typed into real Chrome and never passes through the agent or the model. The resulting cookies live in the station's browser profile, so later runs stay signed in without ever having handled your credentials."},{"id":"mcp-connectors","t":"MCP connectors","b":"StarNet speaks Model Context Protocol . A curated connector catalog covers popular services with one-click setup; connectors that need auth use OAuth 2.1 dynamic client registration or a pasted key. Any third-party remote http(s) MCP server can be added by URL — your agents' reach grows with the ecosystem, not with our release schedule. Local stdio servers ( npx … , uvx … ) are supported only through an agent-owned SAFE CELL . StarNet probes Docker before connecting, runs the server inside that isolated cell, keeps connector secrets out of process arguments, and never falls back to spawning the server directly on your host. If no real Safe Cell agent exists, the local-server route is unavailable rather than pretending the process is isolated. Connecting a specific service? CONNECT A PLATFORM walks the whole decision end to end — which of the two routes applies, how a key is stored and substituted without the model ever seeing it, unattended use, and what each failure message means."},{"id":"messaging-channels","t":"Messaging channels","b":"Connect Telegram , Discord , Slack , Matrix , or Signal and your agents can report to you — and take tasking from you — where you already are. Scheduled and Night Shift runs can deliver their results straight to a channel. Matrix talks to whatever homeserver you point it at, and Signal talks to a signal-cli bridge you run yourself; StarNet never picks the server for you. Outbound messages are a capability like any other: an agent can only write to a chat someone has already opened with this station , never to an arbitrary address it decides on."},{"id":"telegram-as-a-remote-control","t":"Telegram as a remote control","b":"Telegram goes further than notifications. From your phone you can hold a real threaded conversation with a specific agent, start and steer runs, answer an approval an agent is waiting on, and orchestrate the crew — the same command surface as COMMS, reachable when you're nowhere near the machine. The station is still doing the work locally; Telegram is just the window you're looking through."},{"id":"harness-interop","t":"Harness interop","b":"Already run Claude Code, or another agent harness? StarNet exposes an MCP stdio bridge and an OpenAI-style ingress endpoint, so other tools can drive StarNet capabilities — and StarNet agents can sit alongside the harnesses you already use instead of replacing them. Your station becomes shared infrastructure, not another silo."},{"id":"local-by-default","t":"Local by default","b":"Every connector and channel is opt-in. The default station touches the network only for the model provider you configured; anything wider is a grant you made on purpose and can revoke in Settings."}]},{"u":"getting-started.html","t":"Install & first task","g":"Start here","k":"Quickstart","d":"","h":[{"id":"download-and-install","t":"Download and install"},{"id":"windows-first-run","t":"Windows first run"},{"id":"macos-first-run","t":"macOS first run"},{"id":"connect-a-provider","t":"Connect a provider"},{"id":"awaken-your-first-agent","t":"Send your first task"},{"id":"updates","t":"Updates"},{"id":"run-from-source","t":"Run from source"}],"s":[{"id":"download-and-install","t":"Download and install","b":"Download StarNet for Windows or macOS from the releases page : Platform Asset Windows 10 / 11 (64-bit) StarNet_ version _x64-setup.exe macOS — Apple Silicon (M1–M4) StarNet_ version _aarch64.dmg macOS — Intel StarNet_ version _x64.dmg Not sure which Mac you have? Apple menu About This Mac . \"Apple M…\" means Apple Silicon; \"Intel\" means Intel. The wrong one won't run."},{"id":"windows-first-run","t":"Windows first run","b":"Open the installer and follow its steps. If SmartScreen shows \"Windows protected your PC\" , Open More info , verify the publisher matches the release notes, and proceed only then."},{"id":"macos-first-run","t":"macOS first run","b":"Open the .dmg and drag StarNet into Applications . Open StarNet from Applications. If Gatekeeper reports an unknown developer, missing notarization, or a damaged app, stop and report the release tag and asset name. Do not clear quarantine for a purported public release."},{"id":"connect-a-provider","t":"Connect a provider","b":"First launch has two setup steps: choose your agent’s identity, then connect its model. Use a StarNet account, a supported provider sign-in, an API key, or a local model. The models and providers reference explains each option. Select a model before continuing. Your provider or local model must be available for the agent to answer."},{"id":"awaken-your-first-agent","t":"Send your first task","b":"Your first crew member is the Overseer . Talk to it in COMMS, the station’s chat window. Finish the introduction and open your agent’s COMMS conversation. Send a small request, such as “Give me a three-item checklist for planning my week.” Wait for the completed response. If the provider returns an error, check your model connection before trying a larger task. That is enough to start using StarNet. When you want work to pass through several agents, follow Build your first workflow . To give your crew more tools, see Set up your crew ."},{"id":"updates","t":"Updates","b":"Use the desktop app’s Update Center to check for and install updates. If an update cannot complete, follow the troubleshooting guide and note the version and error shown."},{"id":"run-from-source","t":"Run from source","b":"For a development checkout, install Node.js 18 or later, then run: git clone https://github.com/androoAGI/starnet.git cd starnet npm install npm start # open http://localhost:8787 Keep that terminal running while you use the station. The desktop installer is the simpler path if you do not need a source checkout. For speech setup and fallbacks, see Agents, teams & voice ."}]},{"u":"glossary.html","t":"Glossary","g":"Troubleshooting & help","k":"Reference","d":"The StarNet vocabulary in plain English: agents, bays, belts, lines, filters, joiners, the dossier, memory, Night Shift, the leash, the sidecar, and more.","h":[{"id":"people","t":"People and crew"},{"id":"floor","t":"The floor"},{"id":"belts","t":"Belts and lines"},{"id":"work","t":"Work"},{"id":"learning","t":"Learning"},{"id":"autonomy","t":"Autonomy"},{"id":"plumbing","t":"Plumbing"}],"s":[{"id":"people","t":"People and crew","b":"AGENT One AI worker with a name, a class, a model, a desk, and its own memory. Every sprite on the floor is one. Runs are real model calls with real tools. OVERSEER The first agent you create. It runs the station and recruits every agent after it. It can’t be deleted. COMMANDER You. The dossier the crew keeps on its commander is what it studies you means. CLASS RECRUITMENT BAY A head start, not a cage: a Researcher comes tuned for digging, an Engineer for code. 35 curated classes on the roster, a 20-deep SPECIALIST ARCHIVE , plus any custom classes you build or save from an agent. CREW All your agents together. Gear you place is shared by the whole crew; only a computer is per agent. SKIN The character an agent wears. 36 to pick from, any class, changeable any time in the agent’s CONFIG tab. Cosmetic only. DELEGATION One agent dispatching subtasks to crew it summons, then folding the results back. You watch the whole tree run on the floor."},{"id":"floor","t":"The floor","b":"STATION Your whole world: rooms, hallways, decks, props, belts, and the crew inside it. Its layout is real configuration, not decoration. REFIT REFIT STATION Build mode. Ten tools on keys 0 9 : select, room, hallway, surface, move, delete, prop, belt, dupe, lines. Undo always works. GEAR / PROP A placed object. Some gear grants a real ability to the whole crew: DISH = WEB, INTEL CAB = FILES, WORKBENCH = TERMINAL, CORE = MEMORY, STUDIO = IMAGES, CONSOLE = COMPUTE. Place one of each and the station is fully powered. BAY a desk Where one agent sits. Every bay is another agent working in parallel. An agent’s bay needs a computer in its room, or the floor says NO COMPUTE . COMMS The chat panel where you direct the station and where every beat — rate the work, keep this memory, confirm this quest — arrives. TRUTHFUL TELEMETRY The station’s core law: it never shows state the harness can’t prove. A lit desk is a live run; the timer is the real one; a dark belt really won’t run."},{"id":"belts","t":"Belts and lines","b":"BELT The path work takes across the floor. Laid with the BELT tool ( 7 ): click one machine, then another. Lit means this will run ; dark means something is missing. LINE A whole job’s route from front door to out tray: machines plus the belts between them. Every line has a budget ($2.00 per job unless you raise it). BOX WORK ITEM One job in transit. When you see a box move, an agent is really working right then. The app’s tooltips call it a work item. INBOX the front door Where outside work — a routine, a message from a paired chat — enters and drops onto a belt. Its card holds the line’s name and budget. COMMS orders skip it and land at the agent’s bay. OUTBOX the out tray Where finished work leaves the line. Click it to read and rate finished runs. FILTER a signpost Reads each job’s text and sends it down a lane: CODE, RESEARCH, or EVERYTHING ELSE. Never drops work. Needs a default lane. SPLITTER Fans work across its lanes. Alone: jobs take turns (load-balance). With a JOINER downstream: every lane gets the same job (fan-out). JOINER A barrier that waits for every branch (10 minutes for a late one, adjustable), then sends one merged crate on. MERGER A lane funnel: several belts in, one belt out, every crate rides straight through. Combines lanes, never jobs. Nothing to configure. LOOP The one tile that makes a cycle legal: a DONE lane and a BACK lane, up to 5 passes by default (20 max), optionally until a reviewer says APPROVED. LANE One belt leaving a signpost’s tile. A filter or splitter needs at least two; a joiner needs at least two coming in. LINES / BLUEPRINT LINES tool, key 9 Nineteen ready-made layouts — FRONT DESK, RESEARCH LINE, REVISION LOOP, SORTING OFFICE, PARALLEL CREW, THE GAUNTLET and more — dropped in one click. ROUTING PLAN What your belts compile into: the exact plan the server executes. The box you watch and the run that happens are the same plan."},{"id":"work","t":"Work","b":"RUN One agent doing one job: a real model call with real tools, a real timer, and a real cost line in the ledger. DELIVERY What a run produced — a file, a report, a reply. Lands in the OUTBOX or in COMMS for you to read and rate. RATE THE WORK The beat after any run that did real work: &#9650; nailed it mints XP; &#9670; close and &#9660; missed are noted with no XP and no penalty. RECIPE WORK RECIPES WHAT to run: a ready-made job with a small form. 101 built in; save your own. ROUTINE WORK AUTOMATION WHEN it runs: a job on a schedule. Drops in at the INBOX. Only fires while scheduling is enabled. SKILL BUILD ABILITIES HOW to do it: a procedure an agent consults mid-task when the work matches. 73 built in, more written by agents themselves or installed from a SKILL.md you inspected. TASKS Where live work sits — the queue you can see."},{"id":"learning","t":"Learning","b":"DOSSIER CREW COMMANDER What the station has learned about you, in nine dimensions (identity, stack, goals, working style, standing orders, pain points, ambitions, people, schedule). Every agent’s briefing folds it in. Local-first; pin, edit, or forget anything. BELIEF One thing the dossier holds, traced to where it came from. Proposed after runs; you Keep , Edit , or Discard . MEMORY CORE AGENT DOSSIER MEMORY An agent’s own long-term notebook. Each memory traces to the run that earned it. Trust grows with use and halves every 30 days unused; low trust is de-weighted, never silently dropped. XP / LEVEL Minted only by your nailed it verdict. Levels record what shipped and gate nothing. Level 2 at 50 XP, 5 at 500, 10 at 2,250. QUEST LOG Your goals broken into 3 5 milestones, advanced only by real completed work. &#9670; YOU ARE HERE marks the open step. RECOMMENDER Suggests one thing at a time — a recipe, a class, a bespoke specialist — and only with evidence. A suggestion that can’t say why is dropped."},{"id":"autonomy","t":"Autonomy","b":"NIGHT SHIFT The station choosing a small grounded job while you’re away (30 minutes idle), about every 45 minutes, inside your leash. Off by default. INITIATIVE Does it start work on its own? WAIT (default) SUGGEST BUILD FREE . REACH How far an unattended action may go. OBSERVE (read only) SANDBOX (default; nothing leaves the machine) SEND & PUBLISH . PACE / LEASH How many unattended jobs per day: LIGHT 1, STANDARD 3 (default), BUSY 6, MAX 12. MORNING REPORT What COMMS prints when you return: what fired, what was skipped and why, drafts to review. Every line maps to a real record. DECISION LEDGER RECENT DECISIONS An append-only trail of every autonomous fire, skip, and defer with the gate that decided it. BUDGET Hard USD caps — per run, per agent, per day, global — enforced against the real ledger, on top of each line’s own budget."},{"id":"plumbing","t":"Plumbing","b":"SIDECAR The small local server that does the actual work, on 127.0.0.1 . The app window talks to it; nothing talks to a StarNet server. WORKSPACE Where your station state lives on disk: transcripts, memory, ledgers, routines. On Windows under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\StarNet ; on macOS under ~/Library/Application Support . PROVIDER Who runs the model. Eighteen supported, OpenRouter by default; three sign in with no key; Ollama is free and fully local. STARNET is the optional prepaid one. BYOK Bring your own key. Your key, your provider’s bill, real cost shown per run. No markup. LEDGER The append-only record of every run’s true spend. What the cost readouts are read from. CHANNEL A paired chat app — Telegram, Discord, Slack, Matrix, Signal — where your agents appear as contacts and take orders. OWNER PAIRING Telegram only: a one-time 10-minute code you send the bot as /pair , so the first stranger to message it doesn’t own it. CONNECTOR / MCP A way to plug in an outside service (Gmail, a calendar, a docs tool) or to let another harness drive your station. Granted explicitly, never assumed. SERVICE KEYS the KEYS tab Keys for the services your agents call — kept locally, separate from your model provider key. LIVE VOICE Hands-free talking to the station on a speech engine built into the app. No key, no account, your voice never leaves the machine."}]},{"u":"guides/channels.html","t":"Messaging channels","g":"Connect your tools","k":"Guide","d":"Talk to your StarNet agents from Telegram, Discord, Slack, Matrix or Signal: give orders from your phone, stay the only one in control, stop everything remotely.","h":[{"id":"it-s-just-a-chat","t":"It’s just a chat"},{"id":"hook-one-up","t":"Hook one up"},{"id":"staying-in-control","t":"Staying in control"},{"id":"where-next","t":"Where next"}],"s":[{"id":"it-s-just-a-chat","t":"It’s just a chat","b":"Your agents live in your chat app, like any other contact. Message them the way you’d message a person: give an order, ask what’s going on, change your mind halfway. They answer in the same chat, and the finished work lands there too. Meanwhile the actual work happens on your computer at home — the phone is just how you talk to them. Works from Telegram, Discord, Slack, Matrix or Signal — whichever you already use."},{"id":"hook-one-up","t":"Hook one up","b":"Open TOOLSETS Find the channel you want and connect it. Each one walks you through its own short setup. each channel has its own card Prove it’s you The first message pairs your account as the owner . Only the owner can give the station work — strangers who find the bot get nothing. one-time pairing Say something Ask what are you working on? or hand over a task. The reply comes back in the chat, from the agent, like a coworker texting you back. that’s the whole trick"},{"id":"staying-in-control","t":"Staying in control","b":"Only you Your account is paired as the owner. Nobody else can command your station through the channel. Stop a task remotely Send the stop command from your phone to interrupt the current task in that chat. Budgets still bind A job from your phone obeys the same spending caps as one started at the keyboard. // THE 3AM TEST Text an order before bed. Your agents work it overnight. The finished thing is sitting in the chat when you wake up."},{"id":"where-next","t":"Where next","b":"PC FILES WEB YOUR CREW & THEIR GEAR Who you’re texting: your agents, and what the gear you place lets them do. ~4 MIN YOUR FIRST WORKING LINE Optional next step: a one-click setup so bigger jobs flow through several agents. ~4 MIN"}]},{"u":"guides/conveyors.html","t":"Conveyor basics","g":"Conveyor workflows","k":"Guide","d":"Plain-English visual guide: what the boxes on a StarNet belt are, how to build a line, how to tell it is working, and what each message on the floor means.","h":[{"id":"the-whole-idea","t":"The whole idea"},{"id":"why-bother","t":"Why bother?"},{"id":"the-four-pieces","t":"The four pieces"},{"id":"build-one","t":"Build one"},{"id":"is-it-working","t":"Is it working?"},{"id":"four-shapes-cover-everything","t":"Four shapes cover everything"},{"id":"something-s-not-running","t":"Something’s not running"},{"id":"questions-people-actually-ask","t":"Questions people actually ask"}],"s":[{"id":"the-whole-idea","t":"The whole idea","b":"You hand your station a job: put together a report on my competitors. 1 The job shows up as a box on the belt. 2 It reaches the first agent. Their part: dig up the data. 3 What they produced rides on to the next agent. Their part: turn it into the report. 4 The finished report lands in the out tray, ready to use. That’s all a belt is: an assembly line for work. Each agent owns one part of the job and passes their output down the line. And the box is not a cartoon — when you see it move, an agent is really working, right then. Still floor = nothing running. A BOX the job, in transit A DESK one agent, one part of the job A BELT the path the work takes A LINE the whole job, start to finish"},{"id":"why-bother","t":"Why bother?","b":"Each agent owns one part One agent doing the whole job does every part half-well. On a line, the researcher researches, the writer writes, the checker checks. The job runs without you Hand work over at 3am, or have it arrive on a schedule. It moves through the line on its own. You come back to the finished thing. You can watch the work No spinner. No hidden queue. If your job is being worked on, you can see its box moving down the line."},{"id":"the-four-pieces","t":"The four pieces","b":"Open REFIT STATION to build. Everything below is under PROP 6 , and the belt itself is BELT 7 . THE FRONT DOOR called an INBOX Work from outside comes in here. A task you send in. A job on a schedule. A DESK called a BAY One agent sits here. When a box reaches their desk, they do their part of the job. THE OUT TRAY called an OUTBOX Finished work comes out here. Click it to collect it. A SIGNPOST FILTER SPLITTER JOINER LOOP Sits on the belt. Points boxes left or right. // YOU CAN SKIP THE FRONT DOOR Chatting with an agent needs no belts at all. Belts are for real jobs — work with parts, work that should flow through several agents on its own."},{"id":"build-one","t":"Build one","b":"Put down three things Open REFIT STATION . Take the PROP tool 6 . Place a front door, a desk, and an out tray in a row. Leave a small gap between each. IN BAY OUT machines down, gaps left for belt Draw the belt Take the BELT tool 7 . Click the front door. Then click the desk. A belt appears between them. Do the same from the desk to the out tray. click → click BELT tool: two clicks per link Give the desk an agent Click the desk and pick someone. Watch the belt: it lights up. Lit means this will work. CREWED — RUN LIVE a bound bay energizes the run Set a spending cap Click the front door. Give the line a name and a budget. If you skip this, it’s $2.00 per job, and that’s the most it can spend. 6 HOPS · $2.00 the INBOX card holds the caps Try it Close the build screen. Send your station a task. Watch its box come out of the front door, slide to the desk, and drop in. Your agent is on it right now. RUNNING a real crate = a real run // OR SKIP ALL FIVE STEPS The LINES tool 9 drops a whole finished line in one click. See Your First Working Line ."},{"id":"is-it-working","t":"Is it working?","b":"Lit belt = will work. Dark belt = something’s missing. You don’t have to test anything. Every time you change a belt, the app checks it and shows the result on the floor right away. GLOWING This belt ends at a desk with an agent. It moves. Boxes will ride it. DARK Same belt — but the desk is empty. Nothing moves until you fix that. // THE ONE EVERYONE HITS A belt that just touches the corner of a signpost does nothing. The belt must go through the signpost’s square: in one side, out the other."},{"id":"four-shapes-cover-everything","t":"Four shapes cover everything","b":"Same job, four ways to run it through your agents. Each shape is just belts plus one signpost. ONE AFTER ANOTHER HAND-OFF IN AGENT A AGENT B An assembly line. Each agent does their part and passes their output to the next. What comes out is the last agent’s finished work. THIS WAY OR THAT WAY FILTER FILTER code everything else The signpost reads each job and routes it to the right specialist. Code goes to the engineer. Everything else goes to the generalist. ALL AT ONCE SPLITTER SPLITTER A stream of jobs, three agents. The signpost deals them out so all three work at the same time. Nothing gets lost. ROUND AGAIN LOOP LOOP DONE BACK → the earlier bay, up to 5 passes Draft, critique, redraft. The work goes round up to 5 times, then comes out. It can never loop forever or overspend. // THE EXPENSIVE MISTAKE Desk A sends to desk B, and desk B sends back to desk A? That would run forever and bill you forever. The app refuses to run it, and tells you which belt to cut. // A BROKEN STEP NEVER EATS YOUR WORK If a step fails or the budget runs out mid-line, you still get everything finished so far, plus one line telling you where it stopped."},{"id":"something-s-not-running","t":"Something’s not running","b":"When a line won’t run, the reason is written on the machine itself — before anything costs you money. It’s almost always one of these four: NO AGENT — CLICK This desk is empty. Click it and pick an agent. NOT ON THE LINE No belt touches this desk. Connect it with BELT 7 . NOT FED The belts don’t actually reach this desk. Usually one is clipping the corner of a signpost instead of going through it. NOT CONNECTED The front door has no belt out. Click the door, then click a desk. The other 8 messages, and what each one means DUP AGENT — ONE BAY EACH The same agent is at two desks. One desk each. A PROP SITS ON THIS LINE — MOVE IT Furniture is sitting on a belt square. Move the furniture. SPLITTER NEEDS 2 OUT-LANES A splitter needs two ways out. Run belts through it: in one side, out two. JOINER NEEDS 2 IN-LANES A joiner waits for several belts to arrive. It needs at least two coming in. NO DEFAULT LANE — CLICK The filter doesn’t know where everything else goes. Click it and pick a lane. LOOP! — BREAK THE CIRCLE Your belts make a ring. Boxes would circle forever. Delete one square, or add a LOOP tile. WORK LINE LOOPS — CUT ONE HANDOFF Two desks feed each other. That runs forever and bills forever. Cut one belt. LOOP HAS NO DONE / BACK LANE A LOOP tile needs two exits: one going onward, one going back. // NOTHING YOU DO HERE IS PERMANENT DELETE 5 removes anything, and UNDO brings it back. You cannot break your station by experimenting."},{"id":"questions-people-actually-ask","t":"Questions people actually ask","b":"Do I even need belts? No. You can just chat with an agent, no belts anywhere. Belts are for jobs that should flow through several agents on their own — especially ones that arrive while you’re away. My belt is dark. Why? It doesn’t end anywhere useful yet. Follow it to the end. You’ll find an empty desk, a gap, or a belt clipping the corner of a signpost. Can two belts go to the same desk? Yes. Boxes line up and wait their turn. The only thing you can’t do is put one agent at two desks. What if it gets expensive? Every line has a budget — $2.00 per job unless you raise it. At the cap it stops, hands over what it finished, and says so. Does it stop when I close the window? No. The work keeps going. The moving box is just a picture of it — the picture catches up next time you look."}]},{"u":"guides/crew.html","t":"Set up your crew","g":"Station & agents","k":"Guide","d":"Plain-English guide to StarNet agents: recruiting, one desk each, and how placed gear grants real abilities — compute, files, web, memory, terminal, images.","h":[{"id":"getting-an-agent","t":"Getting an agent"},{"id":"one-desk-each","t":"One desk each"},{"id":"gear-powers","t":"Gear = powers"},{"id":"where-next","t":"Where next"}],"s":[{"id":"getting-an-agent","t":"Getting an agent","b":"Hit RECRUIT . Pick a class. That’s it — they walk in. A class is a head start, not a cage: a RESEARCHER comes tuned for digging, a WRITER for drafting, an ENGINEER for code. You can re-spec anyone later from the same door. // NOTHING IS LOCKED There are no levels to grind and no unlocks. A brand-new agent can use everything your station has, from minute one."},{"id":"one-desk-each","t":"One desk each","b":"An agent working on a line needs a desk (a BAY ). One agent per desk, one desk per agent. Want three jobs running at the same time? That’s three agents at three desks."},{"id":"gear-powers","t":"Gear = powers","b":"Furniture isn’t decoration. The right gear switches on a real ability — and one of each is enough for the entire station . CONSOLE grants COMPUTE lets them run at all — no PC, no work INTEL CAB grants FILES reading and writing your files DISH grants WEB searching and reading the web CORE grants MEMORY remembering things between jobs WORKBENCH grants TERMINAL running real code — asks you first STUDIO grants IMAGES making and reading images These are the exact power words the app shows when you place the gear — the palette tile, the placement toast and this table all say the same thing. // PLACE ONE, NOT ONE EACH Gear is shared. One dish, one cabinet, one databank — place it once and your whole crew draws on it. You never furnish per agent, and adding agents never means buying the station again. // THE ONE EXCEPTION Computers. Every agent needs their own PC at their desk — a desk without one shows NO COMPUTE and that agent cannot run. // IF AN AGENT CAN’T DO SOMETHING Check the gear before you blame the agent. No dish = no web. No cabinet = no files. Place the thing and the ability switches on. No restart, no settings."},{"id":"where-next","t":"Where next","b":"HOW CONVEYORS WORK Put your crew on a line so jobs flow through them on their own. CORE &#9679;&#9679; &#9679; ~5 MIN you — anywhere “draft the launch post” the finished work, back in the chat your agent — home, at the station RUN IT FROM YOUR PHONE Hand your crew work from anywhere. ~3 MIN"}]},{"u":"guides/filter.html","t":"Filter & route work","g":"Conveyor workflows","k":"Guide","d":"Plain-English visual guide to the StarNet FILTER: send code one way and everything else the other, without sorting anything by hand.","h":[{"id":"the-whole-idea","t":"The whole idea"},{"id":"what-it-can-tell-apart","t":"What it can tell apart"},{"id":"build-one","t":"Build one"},{"id":"mid-line-filters-read-the-last-output","t":"Mid-line filters read the last output"},{"id":"something-s-not-running","t":"Something’s not running"},{"id":"questions-people-actually-ask","t":"Questions people actually ask"}],"s":[{"id":"the-whole-idea","t":"The whole idea","b":"You have two specialists. Jobs of both kinds arrive at one door. 1 A job arrives at the INBOX and rides the belt. 2 It reaches the FILTER . The filter reads the job. 3 Code goes east to the engineer. Everything else goes south to the generalist. 4 The right specialist does the work. You never touched it. A filter is a signpost on the belt. It reads each job and points it down one lane. It never holds work, never drops work, and never asks you anything once it’s set."},{"id":"what-it-can-tell-apart","t":"What it can tell apart","b":"The filter reads the text of the job and calls it one of exactly three things: CODE write, fix, refactor, ship RESEARCH find out, compare, dig up EVERYTHING ELSE the default lane A LANE one belt leaving the tile If a job reads as both code and research, code wins. If it reads as neither, it takes the EVERYTHING ELSE lane. That lane is required — without it the tile shows NO DEFAULT LANE — CLICK until you pick one. // ADDRESSED WORK SKIPS THE FILTER A job you sent to a named agent already knows where it’s going. It rides straight to that agent’s bay and the filter never sees it. Filters sort the unaddressed work — the jobs that just arrive."},{"id":"build-one","t":"Build one","b":"Open REFIT STATION . Everything below is under PROP 6 , belts are BELT 7 . Put down four things Place an INBOX , a FILTER , and two BAYs — one to the east of the filter, one below it. Leave gaps for belt. INBOX FILTER BAY A BAY B machines down, gaps left for belt Run the first lane Take BELT 7 . Click the INBOX, then the FILTER. Then click the FILTER, then BAY A. The belt runs through the filter’s square: in one side, out the other. INBOX FILTER BAY A BAY B INBOX → FILTER → BAY A, straight east Run the second lane Click the FILTER, then BAY B. The belt leaves the filter’s square on a different side and turns toward BAY B. Two belts out of one tile = two lanes. INBOX FILTER BAY A BAY B a second exit, south, turning east Tell the filter what goes where Click the FILTER tile. Point CODE at the engineer’s lane and EVERYTHING ELSE at the generalist’s. RESEARCH can go either way. Hit &#9656; SAVE ROUTES . &#9646; FILTER — route by content CODE EAST BAY A RESEARCH SOUTH BAY B EVERYTHING ELSE SOUTH BAY B &#9656; SAVE ROUTES three rows, one lane each Give each bay an agent. When both belts glow, the filter is live. Send a coding job and a shopping-list job and watch them part ways at the tile. // OR SKIP ALL FOUR STEPS The LINES tool 9 has two ready-made filter layouts: SORTING OFFICE and TRIAGE DESK . One click drops the whole thing."},{"id":"mid-line-filters-read-the-last-output","t":"Mid-line filters read the last output","b":"A filter doesn’t have to sit right after the INBOX. Put one between two desks and it sorts what the first desk produced — not the original message. A researcher whose notes turn out to be a code problem can hand off to the engineer without anyone deciding that in advance."},{"id":"something-s-not-running","t":"Something’s not running","b":"NO DEFAULT LANE — CLICK The filter doesn’t know where everything else goes. Click the tile and pick a lane for EVERYTHING ELSE. NOT ON A BELT — MOVE IT The filter tile has no belt through it. Move it onto the line, or run the belt through its square. NOT FED A belt is clipping the corner of the filter instead of going through. In one side, out the other. NO AGENT — CLICK One of the destination desks is empty. A lane to an empty desk stays dark."},{"id":"questions-people-actually-ask","t":"Questions people actually ask","b":"Can I filter on my own keywords? Not yet. The filter knows three kinds of work: code, research, everything else. For anything finer, put a specialist first in line and let their output decide the lane — a mid-line filter reads what they produced. What happens to a job the filter can’t classify? It takes the EVERYTHING ELSE lane. A filter never drops work and never parks it. Can one filter have three lanes? Yes. Run three belts out of the tile on three different sides. CODE, RESEARCH, and EVERYTHING ELSE can each have their own lane. Does the filter cost anything? No. Sorting is done by the station itself, not by a model call. Only the desk that does the work spends money."}]},{"u":"guides/first-line.html","t":"Build your first workflow","g":"Conveyor workflows","k":"Guide","d":"Stamp a pre-wired StarNet work line in one click, crew it, and watch a real job ride the belt. The four starter blueprints explained.","h":[{"id":"five-clicks-start-to-finish","t":"Five clicks, start to finish"},{"id":"the-four-ready-made-lines","t":"The four ready-made lines"},{"id":"where-next","t":"Where next"}],"s":[{"id":"five-clicks-start-to-finish","t":"Five clicks, start to finish","b":"Open the build screen Hit REFIT STATION , then the LINES tool 9 . Four ready-made lines appear, each with a little picture. IN BAY OUT four ready-made lines to pick from Pick one Got one agent? Pick SHIP-OUT LOOP . Got two? Pick RESEARCH LINE . You can change anything about it later. click → click pick by how many agents you have Click an empty spot The whole line lands at once — machines and belts, already connected. Nothing to wire up. CREWED — RUN LIVE everything lands already connected Give the desks agents Each empty desk says NO AGENT — CLICK . So: click it, pick someone. That’s the only thing left to do. 6 HOPS · $2.00 click each desk, pick someone Try it Close the build screen and hand your station a job. Watch it ride the line and come back finished. RUNNING a real box, a real job // ROLES ARE SUGGESTIONS, NOT LOCKS Each desk comes with a suggestion — this one wants a RESEARCHER , that one a WRITER . It’s only a suggestion. Put anyone anywhere."},{"id":"the-four-ready-made-lines","t":"The four ready-made lines","b":"SHIP-OUT LOOP 7 2 BAY OUTBOX DESK OUT TRAY The smallest one. One agent; everything they finish comes out the tray. Start here if you have one agent. RESEARCH LINE 17 2 IN AGENT A AGENT B DOOR DESK DESK OUT TRAY A job comes in, one agent researches it, the next writes it up, the finished work comes out. SORTING OFFICE 9 5 FILTER code everything else DOOR SIGNPOST 2 DESKS Coding jobs go to one desk, everything else to the other. The sorting is set up for you. PARALLEL CREW 10 8 SPLITTER DOOR SIGNPOST 3 DESKS A stream of jobs, three agents, all working at once. // GIVE IT ROOM A ready-made line won’t drop on top of your furniture. If the click refuses, find a clearer spot or move the furniture first."},{"id":"where-next","t":"Where next","b":"HOW CONVEYORS WORK Now see how what you just placed actually works, one picture at a time. START HERE &#9679; &#9679; &#9679; ~5 MIN ALL GUIDES The rest of the Field Manual. BOARD"}]},{"u":"guides/goals.html","t":"Goals, quests & XP","g":"Station & agents","k":"Guide","d":"Plain-English guide to StarNet goals: tell the station a real goal, get it broken into milestone quests advanced only by real work, and rate finished runs to mint XP.","h":[{"id":"the-whole-idea","t":"The whole idea"},{"id":"set-a-goal","t":"Set a goal"},{"id":"read-the-quest-log","t":"Read the quest log"},{"id":"how-xp-is-earned","t":"How XP is earned"},{"id":"levels-gate-nothing","t":"Levels gate nothing"},{"id":"questions-people-actually-ask","t":"Questions people actually ask"}],"s":[{"id":"the-whole-idea","t":"The whole idea","b":"I want to go freelance by spring. That’s a goal. The station turns it into a quest. 1 You tell the station a real goal — in your own words, in the COMMANDER dossier. 2 It proposes 3 to 5 milestones and asks in COMMS: here’s how I’d break this down — good? 3 You accept a step . That launches a real job. Finishing the job finishes the step. 4 You rate the work . A good verdict mints XP. Levels record what actually shipped. Nothing here is a to-do list you tick. A milestone completes when the station does the work for it — and the run that did it is written onto the step as evidence."},{"id":"set-a-goal","t":"Set a goal","b":"From the dossier Open &#9787; CREW &#9672; COMMANDER . Hit &#9656; LET THE STATION GET TO KNOW YOU (or REFINE WHAT THE STATION KNOWS ) and answer in plain words. Anything you say about what you want becomes a goal belief. From the quest log Open the QUEST LOG . With no goal set it shows &#9654; SET A GOAL , which takes you to the same place. Once a goal is reached, it offers SET THE NEXT GOAL . Then wait a beat The station reads the new goal, drafts a milestone tree, and offers it once in COMMS after your next run. Confirm it, and the tree appears in the QUEST LOG. // ONE GOAL, WRITTEN LIKE A PERSON The station decomposes what you actually said. Get three paying clients for my design work by March makes a better quest than grow business . If a milestone is wrong, edit the belief and the tree is redrawn."},{"id":"read-the-quest-log","t":"Read the quest log","b":"&#10003; done — a real run completed it &#9670; YOU ARE HERE the one open step RUNNING that step is live right now 1 2 3 the steps still ahead Under the current step is one button: &#9654; ACCEPT THIS STEP . It launches the step as a real job on your crew — the same path any task takes. When that job finishes, the step is marked done and the run’s summary line is stapled to it. There is no manual tick, on purpose."},{"id":"how-xp-is-earned","t":"How XP is earned","b":"Exactly one thing mints XP: you saying a finished run nailed it. After any run that did real work, COMMS offers RATE THE WORK with three buttons: &#9650; NAILED IT &#9733; +XP &#9670; CLOSE noted — no XP, no penalty &#9660; MISSED noted — no XP, no penalty One verdict is worth one fixed amount. It does not scale with how many tools ran or how much was spent — a cheap job you loved is worth the same as an expensive one. On a crew run, every agent that provably did part of the work gets the same verdict. Level Total XP 2 50 3 150 5 500 10 2,250 The other meters on an agent — CONFIDENCE (how satisfied you’ve been lately), RELIABILITY (how often runs complete cleanly), PRACTICE — describe the agent. They mint nothing."},{"id":"levels-gate-nothing","t":"Levels gate nothing","b":"The STATION level at the top of the screen is every agent’s record rolled up. Its own tooltip says it: a level never unlocks or gates anything. Every tool, model, and permission is available from minute one. Levels are proof of real work you rated well — nothing more, nothing less. // THE ONE HONEST EXCEPTION An agent’s own level decides when it may offer you more autonomy: an initiative offer needs level 3, a permission-grant offer needs level 5. The offer is gated. The capability still needs your tap. Nothing happens because a number went up."},{"id":"questions-people-actually-ask","t":"Questions people actually ask","b":"Can I have more than one goal? Yes. Each goal belief with a confirmed tree shows in the QUEST LOG. The station offers one tree at a time so you are never asked to confirm five things at once. What if I forget the goal or change my mind? Forget the belief in the COMMANDER dossier. The whole tree is retired — kept for history, hidden from the log. Does goal progress raise the station level? No. Goals and XP are separate systems. Finishing a milestone marks the step done; only your RATE THE WORK verdict mints XP. Can I give XP by hand? No. If you could, the level would stop meaning anything."}]},{"u":"guides/index.html","t":"Step-by-step guides","g":"Start here","k":"Overview","d":"Visual step-by-step StarNet guides: how conveyor lines work, how to build one, and what every fault label on the floor means.","h":[{"id":"get-your-crew-working","t":"Get your crew working"},{"id":"route-work-across-the-station","t":"Route work across the station"},{"id":"make-it-an-ongoing-routine","t":"Make it an ongoing routine"}],"s":[{"id":"get-your-crew-working","t":"Get your crew working","b":"Start with a person and a task, then build your first line. Set up your crew Recruit agents and place the gear that gives them real capabilities. Build your first workflow Place a ready-made line and send it a real job. Work toward a goal Turn goals into milestones and understand quests and XP. 02"},{"id":"route-work-across-the-station","t":"Route work across the station","b":"Understand each piece of a conveyor workflow. Conveyor basics Follow a job from its input to its output, and read the floor’s status labels. Filter & route work Send different kinds of work to different agents. Split & combine results Run several branches, wait for their results, and bring them together. 03"},{"id":"make-it-an-ongoing-routine","t":"Make it an ongoing routine","b":"Repeat useful work and reach the station while you’re away. Create repeatable work Choose what runs, how it runs, and when it runs. Set up Night Shift Configure unattended work and read the morning report. Message your station Connect a messaging channel and send tasks from your phone. Looking up a setting? Find the reference pages alongside their related guides. Browse all topics"}]},{"u":"guides/night-shift.html","t":"Set up Night Shift","g":"Repeat & automate","k":"Guide","d":"Plain-English guide to StarNet Night Shift: how the station works while you are away, the three-part leash you set, what the morning report contains, and how to read the decision trail.","h":[{"id":"the-whole-idea","t":"The whole idea"},{"id":"the-three-part-leash","t":"The three-part leash"},{"id":"point-it-somewhere","t":"Point it somewhere"},{"id":"the-six-gates","t":"The six gates"},{"id":"the-morning-report","t":"The morning report"},{"id":"the-receipt-recent-decisions","t":"The receipt: RECENT DECISIONS"},{"id":"questions-people-actually-ask","t":"Questions people actually ask"}],"s":[{"id":"the-whole-idea","t":"The whole idea","b":"You go to bed. The station picks a small, grounded job toward your goals, does it, and leaves a draft on your desk. 1 You’ve been away 30 minutes (no clicks, no keys). The station counts you as gone. 2 About every 45 minutes it considers one job. It checks six gates first. Most nights, most beats are skipped. 3 A job that passes runs for real — inside your REACH setting, under your budget caps. 4 You come back to a morning report : what fired, what was skipped and why, drafts to review. Out of the box the station does nothing unattended. INITIATIVE starts at WAIT. Everything below is you choosing to turn a dial."},{"id":"the-three-part-leash","t":"The three-part leash","b":"Open SETTINGS AUTONOMY . Three rows: INITIATIVE — does it start work on its own? WAIT nothing runs unless you ask (default) SUGGEST lines up suggestions you approve — never acts on its own BUILD does a few small grounded jobs a day on its own FREE picks & does work toward your goals while you’re away REACH — how far an unattended action may go OBSERVE read / research only — writes nothing SANDBOX build & write locally — nothing leaves the machine (default) SEND & PUBLISH the highest rung: unattended actions may leave the machine — send, publish, or contact external services PACE — how many unattended jobs per day LIGHT 1 STANDARD 3 (default) BUSY 6 MAX 12 the daily limit, counted per calendar day // FREE DOES NOT MEAN UNLEASHED Even on FREE, the presets keep REACH at SANDBOX. Nothing leaves your machine overnight unless you move REACH to SEND & PUBLISH yourself."},{"id":"point-it-somewhere","t":"Point it somewhere","b":"FOCUS STEER it at a project folder, a thread, or a goal, and every overnight beat starts there. CLEAR hands the choice back to the station. OFF-LIMITS RULE OUT anything it should never touch unattended. The station respects the list before it picks a job. BUDGET SETTINGS BUDGET holds hard USD caps — PER RUN , PER AGENT , PER DAY , GLOBAL — enforced against the real ledger. Night Shift can’t spend past them."},{"id":"the-six-gates","t":"The six gates","b":"Before any beat fires, the station checks, in order: posture (is INITIATIVE above WAIT?), away (have you really been gone 30 minutes?), leash (any jobs left today?), cooldown (45 minutes since the last?), halt (does an older saved automation pause remain?), concurrency (is something already running?). The first gate that says no ends the beat — and that reason is what you read in the morning."},{"id":"the-morning-report","t":"The morning report","b":"The first time you’re back after a genuine absence, COMMS prints one report. Every line maps to a real record: Priority — what it was working toward, marked (you steered this) if you set a FOCUS, and why. Headline — 3 beats fired, 2 drafts on your desk , or 5 beats skipped — nothing landed on your desk . &#10003; one line per draft it produced. Skipped beats, grouped by reason — 2 beats skipped: daily limit reached (by 1:10 AM) , in your local time. Drafts in full when you say show me . If nothing fired, it says so plainly and says why. It never pads a quiet night."},{"id":"the-receipt-recent-decisions","t":"The receipt: RECENT DECISIONS","b":"SETTINGS NIGHT SHIFT shows the live state — STATE , MODE , DAILY LIMIT , LAST JOB , NEXT JOB EARLIEST — plus RECENT DECISIONS : an append-only trail of every fire, skip, and defer, with the gate that decided it. It is written to disk as it happens and can’t be edited after the fact. &#9636; LAST REPORT re-opens the morning report any time. // PAUSE AWAY-WORK Set INITIATIVE to WAIT to prevent new Night Shift jobs. Use Stop in COMMS for the current task. Older saved global pauses can be cleared with RESUME AUTOMATION, without changing permissions or job settings."},{"id":"questions-people-actually-ask","t":"Questions people actually ask","b":"Can it email people while I sleep? Only if you set REACH to SEND & PUBLISH and have granted the tool. On OBSERVE or SANDBOX (the default) nothing leaves the machine. Is there a quiet-hours setting? No clock. Away means no input for 30 minutes, day or night. If you want it to work only while you sleep, leave INITIATIVE at WAIT during the day. What counts as one of my 3 jobs? One beat that fired. Skipped beats don’t count. The counter resets each calendar day. Where do the drafts go? On your desk in COMMS, listed in the morning report, and under DRAFTS in the NIGHT SHIFT panel until you review them. A gentle nudge reminds you if they sit unreviewed."}]},{"u":"guides/routines.html","t":"Create repeatable work","g":"Repeat & automate","k":"Guide","d":"Plain-English guide to the three kinds of repeatable work in StarNet: recipes (what to run), routines (when it runs), and skills (how an agent does it).","h":[{"id":"three-words-three-jobs","t":"Three words, three jobs"},{"id":"recipes-pick-a-job-off-the-shelf","t":"Recipes: pick a job off the shelf"},{"id":"routines-the-same-job-on-a-schedule","t":"Routines: the same job, on a schedule"},{"id":"skills-how-an-agent-does-the-work","t":"Skills: how an agent does the work"},{"id":"how-they-fit-together","t":"How they fit together"},{"id":"questions-people-actually-ask","t":"Questions people actually ask"}],"s":[{"id":"three-words-three-jobs","t":"Three words, three jobs","b":"RECIPE WHAT to run — a ready-made job with a tiny form ROUTINE WHEN it runs — a job on a schedule SKILL HOW to do it — a procedure an agent follows mid-task Every weekday at 7, brief me on my competitors. That’s a recipe (the brief) on a routine (7:00 weekdays), and the researcher may pull a skill (how to source claims properly) while doing it."},{"id":"recipes-pick-a-job-off-the-shelf","t":"Recipes: pick a job off the shelf","b":"Open WORK &#9635; RECIPES . There are 101 built-in recipes in eight shelves — developer , research , creator , ops , business , money , data , general . Pick one, fill in its two or three fields, and it lands on TASKS as a real job for your crew. Save your own &#65291; SAVE A RECIPE turns any job you keep typing into a form. Edit, tweak, or fork a built-in the same way. Put it on a clock Every recipe card has &#9719; MAKE ROUTINE . One click turns the WHAT into a WHEN. Let the station suggest The recommender ranks all 101 against what you actually do and offers one at a time — with the reason, or not at all."},{"id":"routines-the-same-job-on-a-schedule","t":"Routines: the same job, on a schedule","b":"Open WORK &#8734; AUTOMATION and hit + ADD ROUTINE . Write the job (or start from a recipe), pick who runs it, and answer WHEN SHOULD IT RUN? — a morning brief, a nightly summary, a recurring check. Write the job Plain words. Summarize what changed in my inbox since yesterday and flag anything that needs a reply. Choose the agent. ROUTINE every weekday 7:00 INBOX the job drops in BAY RESEARCHER runs the RECIPE OUTBOX the morning brief uses a SKILL if one matches the work the job a routine will drop in Pick the schedule Every weekday at 7. Every Sunday night. Every 2 hours. The picker shows exactly when the next run will be. WHEN SHOULD IT RUN? WEEKDAYS 7:00 next run: tomorrow, 7:00 AM the WHEN picker Arm the scheduler Routines only fire while scheduling is on: &#9654; ENABLE SCHEDULING . Off, a routine is saved but dormant — the panel says &#9675; SCHEDULING IS OFF so nothing is ambiguous. &#9675; SCHEDULING IS OFF &#9654; ENABLE SCHEDULING a saved automation pause can be resumed saved is not the same as armed Where does the job land? At the INBOX . A routine is exactly the outside work the front door exists for — it drops onto your belts and flows through your line like any other job. No belts? It goes straight to the agent’s bay. // THREE MORE DOORS Routines can also be created from an INBOX ’s TRIGGER zone in REFIT, proposed by the station itself when it notices a pattern, or from your phone: /routine add schedule | task in any paired channel."},{"id":"skills-how-an-agent-does-the-work","t":"Skills: how an agent does the work","b":"A skill is a procedure document. Agents don’t launch skills — they consult them when a task matches. Open BUILD &#8644; ABILITIES : Section What’s in it SKILL LIBRARY 73 pre-installed procedures, grouped by kind. A skill stays locked until the agent has the gear it needs — a browsing skill needs the DISH placed. AGENT SKILLS Procedures your agents wrote or learned themselves. They appear as a compact index in future runs; the full body loads only when a task matches. SKILL EXCHANGE Paste an HTTPS or GitHub link to a SKILL.md and hit INSPECT — you see provenance and a guard review before anything installs. Or import an exported package. There is no blank write a skill form. Skills enter the station two ways: an agent writes one from experience, or you install one you’ve inspected. A routine can name up to 8 skills its agent should have on hand."},{"id":"how-they-fit-together","t":"How they fit together","b":"1 A ROUTINE fires at 7:00 and drops a job at the INBOX. 2 The job is a RECIPE — competitor brief with your three fields filled in. 3 The researcher pulls a matching SKILL mid-run — how to source every claim. 4 The brief lands in the OUTBOX. You rate it ; the next run is built from what you valued."},{"id":"questions-people-actually-ask","t":"Questions people actually ask","b":"Does a routine run if the app is closed? No. The station is local-first — the scheduler lives in the app on your machine. Leave it running (it’s happy minimized) and routines fire on time. Can a routine run through a whole conveyor line? Yes. It drops in at the INBOX and follows your belts through every desk, filter, and joiner like any other job. What’s the difference between a routine and Night Shift? A routine is a job you defined on a clock you set. Night Shift is the station choosing its own small job while you’re away, inside a leash. Routines run whether you’re there or not. Where do my custom recipes live? On your machine, with the rest of your station state. Nothing you save is uploaded anywhere."}]},{"u":"guides/splitter-joiner.html","t":"Split & combine results","g":"Conveyor workflows","k":"Guide","d":"Plain-English visual guide to the StarNet SPLITTER and JOINER: give one job to several agents at once, then wait for all of them and merge what they send back.","h":[{"id":"the-whole-idea","t":"The whole idea"},{"id":"a-splitter-on-its-own-shares-the-load","t":"A splitter on its own shares the load"},{"id":"build-one","t":"Build one"},{"id":"the-merger-is-not-a-joiner","t":"The merger is not a joiner"},{"id":"something-s-not-running","t":"Something’s not running"},{"id":"questions-people-actually-ask","t":"Questions people actually ask"}],"s":[{"id":"the-whole-idea","t":"The whole idea","b":"Give me three takes on this pitch. One job, three analysts, one combined answer. 1 The job arrives at the INBOX and reaches the SPLITTER . 2 The splitter sends a copy down every lane . Three desks start at the same time. 3 Each result rides to the JOINER . The joiner waits until all three are in. 4 It sends one merged crate onward — to a writer, or straight to the OUTBOX. Two signposts, two jobs. The SPLITTER fans work out. The JOINER is a barrier: nothing leaves it until every branch has reported in."},{"id":"a-splitter-on-its-own-shares-the-load","t":"A splitter on its own shares the load","b":"INBOX SPLITTER BAY BAY job 1, 3, 5 job 2, 4, 6 NO JOINER: TAKE TURNS Each job goes down one lane, in turn: first east, then south, then east again. Two desks, half the queue — not double the spend. INBOX SPLITTER BAY BAY JOINER same job same job WITH A JOINER: EVERYONE If the lanes meet at a joiner downstream, the splitter switches to fan-out : every lane gets the same job. Every branch is a real run, so this stage costs once per desk. You don’t choose between the two modes. The station reads your belts: lanes that meet at a joiner mean everyone works on it ; lanes that don’t mean take turns ."},{"id":"build-one","t":"Build one","b":"Lay out the crew Open REFIT STATION . Place an INBOX , a SPLITTER , two or three BAYs side by side, a JOINER past them, and an OUTBOX . INBOX SPLITTER BAY BAY JOINER same job same job the shape you are building toward Fan out BELT 7 : INBOX SPLITTER, then SPLITTER each BAY. Every belt must leave the splitter’s square on its own side . Two lanes minimum, or the tile says SPLITTER NEEDS 2 OUT-LANES . INBOX SPLITTER BAY BAY job 1, 3, 5 job 2, 4, 6 one belt out per side Fan back in BELT each BAY JOINER, into the joiner’s square on different sides. Two lanes minimum, or the tile says JOINER NEEDS 2 IN-LANES . Then JOINER OUTBOX. INBOX one job in SPLITTER BAY ANALYST works its copy BAY ANALYST works its copy JOINER OUTBOX one merged answer copy 1 copy 2 every branch comes home Decide how long to wait Click the JOINER. The default is 10 minutes for a late branch (you can set 1 to 120). If a branch is slower than that, the joiner sends what it has and marks the result partial . &#9646; JOINER — BRANCHES WAIT TIMEOUT — PARTIAL RELEASE 10 minutes to wait for a late branch waits for every branch then sends ONE crate on the JOINER card Give every bay an agent. Send one job. Watch three crates leave the splitter, three desks light up, and one crate leave the joiner. // OR SKIP ALL FOUR STEPS LINES 9 has four ready-made crews: PARALLEL CREW , RESEARCH SWARM , SECOND OPINION , and LOAD BALANCER ."},{"id":"the-merger-is-not-a-joiner","t":"The merger is not a joiner","b":"There is a third tile called MERGER . It looks similar and does something much simpler: several belts come in, one belt goes out, and every crate rides straight through . It combines lanes , never jobs . Nothing to configure. Use it when two lines share one OUTBOX; use a JOINER when results must be combined. // THE COST OF A CREW Fan-out means every branch is a real model run. Three desks, three runs, three bills for that stage. Your line budget (the INBOX card, $2.00 per job unless you raise it) still caps the whole job — if it runs out mid-crew, you get what finished plus a note saying where it stopped."},{"id":"something-s-not-running","t":"Something’s not running","b":"SPLITTER NEEDS 2 OUT-LANES Only one belt leaves the splitter. Run a second belt out of its square on another side. JOINER NEEDS 2 IN-LANES Only one belt reaches the joiner. Run a second belt into its square. NOT FED A belt clips a corner of the splitter or joiner instead of passing through the square. NO AGENT — CLICK One desk in the crew is empty. The joiner would wait forever for it, so the lane stays dark until you fill the desk."},{"id":"questions-people-actually-ask","t":"Questions people actually ask","b":"Do all three agents need to be the same class? No. Three different specialists on the same job is the whole point of SECOND OPINION: an analyst, a skeptic, and a writer each read the same brief. What does the merged crate contain? Every branch’s result, labelled by desk, in one crate. Put a writer after the joiner to turn three takes into one document. What if I restart the app while a joiner is waiting? The joiner’s waiting room is not saved across a restart — on purpose, so a stale half-job can never surprise you a week later. Parked branches are reported at boot and the job is marked superseded. Send it again. Can I split, then split again? Yes. A splitter downstream of a splitter fans out again, and one joiner at the end still waits for every leaf."}]},{"u":"help.html","t":"Help center","g":"Troubleshooting & help","k":"Help","d":"StarNet help center: the fastest route to an answer — picture guides, troubleshooting, keyboard shortcuts, glossary, common questions, and how to reach a human.","h":[{"id":"help-inside-the-app","t":"Help inside the app"},{"id":"common-questions","t":"Common questions"},{"id":"installing","t":"Installing"},{"id":"keys-models-and-cost","t":"Keys, models and cost"},{"id":"belts-and-lines","t":"Belts and lines"},{"id":"agents-and-the-station","t":"Agents and the station"},{"id":"night-shift-and-phones","t":"Night Shift and phones"},{"id":"reach-a-human","t":"Reach a human"}],"s":[{"id":"help-inside-the-app","t":"Help inside the app","b":"Open &#9635; SYSTEM FIELD MANUAL . The FIRST STEPS tab has a REPLAY QUICK TOUR button that re-runs the real onboarding tour — the same one that placed your first gear and ran your first command. Every button in the station also has a tooltip that says what it does and, where one exists, its shortcut."},{"id":"common-questions","t":"Common questions","b":""},{"id":"installing","t":"Installing","b":"Windows says Windows protected your PC SmartScreen can warn while a signing certificate builds reputation. Open More info , check the publisher matches the release notes, and proceed only then. Details in troubleshooting . macOS says the app is damaged or can’t be opened Public Mac builds must pass Apple notarization. A Gatekeeper failure on a real release is something to report, not bypass: androo.agi@gmail.com with the exact message and version. Which Mac download do I need? Apple menu About This Mac . Apple M means Apple Silicon ( aarch64.dmg ); Intel means Intel ( x64.dmg ). The wrong one won’t run. Can I run it without installing? Yes — clone the repo and npm install && npm start with Node 18+. The station opens at localhost:8787 . Keys live in a local file instead of the OS keychain in that mode."},{"id":"keys-models-and-cost","t":"Keys, models and cost","b":"Which providers work? Eighteen, including OpenRouter (the default), OpenAI, Anthropic, Gemini, xAI, Groq, Mistral, DeepSeek, Together, Fireworks, Perplexity, Cerebras, any OpenAI-compatible endpoint, and Ollama (free, fully local). Three need no key at all — ChatGPT, Grok, and Kimi sign in with a device code. Full list in Providers & Keys . Where is my API key stored? In the desktop app: your operating system’s keychain, handed to the local sidecar at start. It is sent only to the provider you chose, from your machine. Nothing goes to a StarNet server. See privacy . How do I stop it spending money? Every conveyor line has a per-job budget ($2.00 unless you raise it). SETTINGS BUDGET adds hard caps per run, per agent, per day, and globally, enforced against the real ledger. Use Stop in COMMS for the current task, and pause scheduled work in its own panel. What do StarNet Credits change? Nothing about how the app works — it’s one more provider, STARNET , that runs on a prepaid balance instead of a key you manage. Linking a station opens an account on the billing service; payment always happens in your browser, never in the app. Optional, never required."},{"id":"belts-and-lines","t":"Belts and lines","b":"Do I need belts at all? No. Chatting with an agent in COMMS needs no belts. Belts are for jobs that should flow through several agents on their own. How conveyors work explains the whole idea in five minutes. The belt is dark and nothing moves Follow it to the end. You’ll find an empty desk ( NO AGENT — CLICK ), a gap, or a belt clipping the corner of a signpost instead of going through it ( NOT FED ). The full list of floor messages is in the conveyor guide . It says WORK LINE LOOPS Two desks feed each other, which would run and bill forever. The station refuses to run it. Cut one belt — or, if you want a revision cycle on purpose, put a LOOP tile on it (up to 5 passes by default, 20 max). I don’t want to build anything The LINES tool ( 9 in REFIT) drops one of 19 finished layouts in a click. Your first working line walks through it."},{"id":"agents-and-the-station","t":"Agents and the station","b":"How many agents can I have? As many as you have bays for. Every bay is another agent running in parallel. Each needs a computer in its room (the floor says NO COMPUTE otherwise); everything else — dish, cabinet, workbench, core — is placed once and shared by the whole crew. Why can’t my agent browse the web / read files? Because the gear isn’t placed. A DISH grants WEB, an INTEL CAB grants FILES, a WORKBENCH grants TERMINAL, a CORE grants MEMORY, a STUDIO grants IMAGES. Place it in REFIT and the ability switches on. Your crew & their gear . How do I rename an agent or change its look? &#9787; CREW &#9673; AGENTS opens the dossier. The &#9998; beside the name renames; the CONFIG tab has a SKIN picker with a live preview. Both are cosmetic — nothing about the agent’s record changes. Does the level unlock anything? No. Levels are proof of work you rated well. Every tool, model, and permission is available from minute one. Goals, quests & XP ."},{"id":"night-shift-and-phones","t":"Night Shift and phones","b":"Will it do things while I’m away? Not until you turn the dial. INITIATIVE starts at WAIT . Even at FREE , REACH stays at SANDBOX — nothing leaves your machine unless you raise it yourself. Night Shift . Can I talk to my agents from my phone? Yes — pair Telegram, Discord, Slack, Matrix, or Signal. Your agents show up as contacts. Run it from your phone . My Telegram bot says DMs BLOCKED Telegram requires owner pairing so the first stranger to DM your bot doesn’t own it. Hit PAIR OWNER in the CHANNELS window, then send /pair plus the one-time code to the bot within 10 minutes."},{"id":"reach-a-human","t":"Reach a human","b":"BUG OR REQUEST GitHub issues Best for anything reproducible. Include your version (bottom of the SYSTEM window) and OS. EMAIL androo.agi@gmail.com Trust failures, install problems, anything you’d rather not post publicly. READ THE SOURCE github.com/androoAGI/starnet MIT licensed. Every claim on this site can be checked against it. House rule: these pages are read off the shipped code, not off a plan. If a page and your station disagree, one of them is a bug — tell us which."}]},{"u":"index.html","t":"Documentation","g":"Start here","k":"Overview","d":"Documentation for installing, configuring, and operating the StarNet local-first AI agent station: getting started, the Field Manual picture guides, reference, and help.","h":[{"id":"first-task","t":"New to StarNet?"},{"id":"browse-topics","t":"Browse by topic"},{"id":"start-title","t":"Start here"},{"id":"station-title","t":"Station & agents"},{"id":"workflows-title","t":"Conveyor workflows"},{"id":"automation-title","t":"Repeat & automate"},{"id":"connections-title","t":"Connect your tools"},{"id":"help-title","t":"Troubleshooting & help"}],"s":[{"id":"first-task","t":"New to StarNet?","b":"Start with one agent. Build from there. 01 / INSTALL Get StarNet Install the app and open your station. Install StarNet 02 / CONNECT Connect a model Use a provider account, API key, or local model. Choose a provider 03 / RUN Run your first task Meet your agent and send a request. Send your first task"},{"id":"browse-topics","t":"Browse by topic","b":"Prefer a walkthrough? 01"},{"id":"start-title","t":"Start here","b":"Install StarNet, choose a model, and give your first agent a task. Install & first task Quickstart Models & providers Reference 02"},{"id":"station-title","t":"Station & agents","b":"Build your station, recruit a crew, and give them the tools to work. Station & capabilities Reference Set up your crew Guide Agents, teams & voice Reference Goals, quests & XP Guide 03"},{"id":"workflows-title","t":"Conveyor workflows","b":"Send work between agents, route different jobs, and combine results. Build your first workflow Guide Conveyor basics Guide Filter & route work Guide Split & combine results Guide 04"},{"id":"automation-title","t":"Repeat & automate","b":"Reuse good work, schedule routines, and configure unattended runs. Create repeatable work Guide Skills & routines reference Reference Set up Night Shift Guide Autonomy controls Reference 05"},{"id":"connections-title","t":"Connect your tools","b":"Connect services, message your station, or work with another harness. Connect a service Guide Messaging channels Guide Connectors & MCP reference Reference From OpenClaw or Hermes Guide 06"},{"id":"help-title","t":"Troubleshooting & help","b":"Fix a problem, look up a shortcut, or get help from a human. Help center Help Troubleshooting Help Keyboard shortcuts Reference Glossary Reference Something not working? Start with the symptom, or get help from a human. Troubleshooting Contact support These docs follow current source. Your installed version may differ. Check the public release notes when a control looks different."}]},{"u":"migrating.html","t":"From OpenClaw or Hermes","g":"Connect your tools","k":"Guide","d":"","h":[{"id":"concept-map","t":"Concept map"},{"id":"you-don-t-have-to-switch","t":"You don't have to switch"},{"id":"what-s-genuinely-different","t":"What's genuinely different"}],"s":[{"id":"concept-map","t":"Concept map","b":"You know it as In StarNet Persona / soul file The agent dossier — personality, beliefs, goals, shaped at recruitment and evolving as you work Skills Skills — same idea, capability-gated by the station floor (a skill that needs an ungranted tool stays visibly dormant) Cron jobs / heartbeats Routines — scheduled real runs that surface as readable sessions afterwards Chat channels Channels — Telegram, Discord, Slack, Matrix, and Signal (via your signal-cli bridge) connect the same way: your bot token, your server One assistant, many contexts Many agents, one station — each with its own model, memory, desk, and tool grants, running concurrently Config files for permissions The floor — placed gear grants real tools, shared by the whole crew; redraw instead of re-config MCP servers MCP connectors — same protocol, curated catalog plus anything you run yourself"},{"id":"you-don-t-have-to-switch","t":"You don't have to switch","b":"StarNet exposes an MCP stdio bridge and an OpenAI-style /v1 ingress endpoint , so the harness you already run can drive StarNet capabilities directly. Keep your daily driver; give it a station to command. Interop details in Connectors & interop ."},{"id":"what-s-genuinely-different","t":"What's genuinely different","b":"Truthful telemetry. The station renders only provable harness state — the visual layer is an instrument, not a costume. The layout is the permission system. Capability boundaries you can see and redraw, not config you have to remember. Concurrency as furniture. More bays = more genuinely parallel bounded runs. Agent importer: the Recruitment Bay has a built-in importer that carries an existing OpenClaw or Hermes agent (persona, memory, config) into a StarNet dossier — point it at your agent's folder and it walks the rest."}]},{"u":"providers.html","t":"Models & providers","g":"Start here","k":"Reference","d":"Model providers supported by StarNet, including API-key, account sign-in, local, and custom OpenAI-compatible options.","h":[{"id":"bring-your-own-key-byok","t":"Bring your own key (BYOK)"},{"id":"supported-providers","t":"Supported providers"},{"id":"where-keys-live","t":"Where keys live"},{"id":"service-keys-the-keys-tab","t":"Service keys (the KEYS tab)"},{"id":"cost-honesty","t":"Cost honesty"}],"s":[{"id":"bring-your-own-key-byok","t":"Bring your own key (BYOK)","b":"StarNet talks to models through your credentials. The simplest path is an OpenRouter API key: one key, hundreds of models, transparent per-token pricing. Paste it once in Settings and every agent can use any model you allow."},{"id":"supported-providers","t":"Supported providers","b":"The provider picker is a first-class roster, not one generic endpoint wearing different labels: Account sign-in: ChatGPT (Codex), Grok, and Kimi for Coding. These use the provider's sign-in flow instead of asking you to paste an API key. Direct API keys: OpenRouter, OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, xAI, Groq, Mistral, DeepSeek, Together AI, Fireworks AI, Perplexity, and Cerebras. Local and custom: Ollama on your machine, or any custom endpoint that speaks the OpenAI-compatible wire format. Release note: these docs track current source. The stable installer linked from the site can lag while the next Windows and macOS builds pass signing and notarization."},{"id":"where-keys-live","t":"Where keys live","b":"Keys are stored locally in your OS keychain via the desktop shell — never in plain project files, never sent anywhere except the provider they belong to. Provider requests leave your machine only when an agent actually runs."},{"id":"service-keys-the-keys-tab","t":"Service keys (the KEYS tab)","b":"Beyond model providers, agents sometimes need platform keys to do real work — a Stripe test key, a GitHub token, a search API key. The KEYS tab lets you paste any service key; StarNet exposes it to agent runs as an environment variable and tells the agent only the name of what's available, never the value. The key itself never enters the model prompt."},{"id":"cost-honesty","t":"Cost honesty","b":"Every run records what it actually cost, from the provider's own usage numbers. The ledger shows real spend per agent and per task — there is no abstraction layer marking up your tokens, and no meter that guesses."}]},{"u":"shortcuts.html","t":"Keyboard shortcuts","g":"Troubleshooting & help","k":"Reference","d":"Every keyboard shortcut StarNet actually binds: fullscreen, the ten REFIT build tools, undo and redo, camera keys, and window navigation.","h":[{"id":"anywhere-in-the-station","t":"Anywhere in the station"},{"id":"refit-station-build-mode","t":"REFIT STATION (build mode)"},{"id":"editing-and-camera-inside-refit","t":"Editing and camera, inside REFIT"},{"id":"comms-and-windows","t":"COMMS and windows"},{"id":"on-these-docs-pages","t":"On these docs pages"}],"s":[{"id":"anywhere-in-the-station","t":"Anywhere in the station","b":"Toggle fullscreen F11 Close the open window (inside a text field, blurs the field first) Esc Leave CINEMA camera mode Esc End a Live Voice session Esc Advance a dialogue beat (also skips the typewriter) Space / Enter Move focus between controls inside a window Tab / Shift + Tab"},{"id":"refit-station-build-mode","t":"REFIT STATION (build mode)","b":"These are live only while REFIT is open. Open it from the bottom bar: &#9874; BUILD REFIT STATION . There is no hotkey to open REFIT itself. SELECT — click a machine to open it 0 ROOM — click to place, drag to size 1 HALLWAY — click to run, drag to size 2 SURFACE — click a room to lay this deck 3 MOVE — drag a room or machine 4 DELETE — click anything; drag across a belt to clear the run 5 PROP — click the deck to place it 6 BELT — click one machine, then another 7 DUPE — click a thing to copy it 8 LINES — pick a ready-made line, then click the deck 9"},{"id":"editing-and-camera-inside-refit","t":"Editing and camera, inside REFIT","b":"Undo Ctrl + Z Redo Ctrl + Y or Ctrl + Shift + Z Turn the prop under the cursor forward / back R / Shift + R Flip the prop under the cursor M Fit the camera to the station F Pan the camera (hold and drag) Space Zoom mouse wheel Step back: close card cancel drag drop half-made belt back to SELECT close REFIT Esc On a Mac, Cmd works wherever Ctrl is listed. Belts have no rotate key: re-lay a belt over an existing one and it re-aims in place; R and M turn props, not belts."},{"id":"comms-and-windows","t":"COMMS and windows","b":"Complete a slash command in the composer Tab Run the slash command Enter On a consent prompt: use your judgment (ask) / deny (permission) Esc Close an image lightbox Esc RECRUITMENT BAY: focus the class search / RECRUITMENT BAY: step back a view / close Esc Workstream rail: move, jump to ends, open Home End Enter Workstream / project row menu Shift + F10"},{"id":"on-these-docs-pages","t":"On these docs pages","b":"Search the docs / Previous / next page [ / ]"}]},{"u":"skills.html","t":"Skills & routines reference","g":"Repeat & automate","k":"Reference","d":"","h":[{"id":"the-three-layer-model","t":"The three-layer model"},{"id":"skills","t":"Skills"},{"id":"recipes","t":"Recipes"},{"id":"routines","t":"Routines"},{"id":"the-loop-closes-work-that-sharpens-itself","t":"The loop closes: work that sharpens itself"}],"s":[{"id":"the-three-layer-model","t":"The three-layer model","b":"Layer Answers Example Skill HOW to do something well \"how we write release notes\" Recipe WHAT to produce \"weekly competitor digest\" Routine WHEN it happens \"every Monday, 9am\" Skills teach technique, recipes define deliverables built from those techniques, and routines put recipes on a schedule. Each layer is inspectable and editable — no black-box \"automations.\""},{"id":"skills","t":"Skills","b":"A skill is a packaged instruction set an agent loads when the task calls for it. Agents ship with class-appropriate skills, and gain more as you work together. Skills are capability-gated : a skill that needs a tool the agent hasn't been granted stays visibly dormant instead of silently failing — the skills panel always distinguishes what you chose to grant from what the station floor provides."},{"id":"recipes","t":"Recipes","b":"A recipe captures a deliverable you'll want again: its inputs, its steps, its output format. Run one on demand, hand it to any capable agent, or attach it to a routine. Your usual recipe is offered as a one-tap prefill when you return to a session."},{"id":"routines","t":"Routines","b":"Routines are the schedule layer — see Autonomy & Night Shift for how scheduled runs execute and report. A routine is always a real recipe run by a real agent; there is no separate, weaker \"automation mode.\""},{"id":"the-loop-closes-work-that-sharpens-itself","t":"The loop closes: work that sharpens itself","b":"Rate a delivered run and the station extracts why it landed (or didn't) into your taste profile. Recipes carry those learnings forward, the quest board regenerates around your current goals instead of stale ones, and the scout studies your history to pitch next moves you didn't think to ask for. A routine left running doesn't decay into noise — each cycle is built with more evidence of what you value than the last."}]},{"u":"station.html","t":"Station & capabilities","g":"Station & agents","k":"Reference","d":"","h":[{"id":"placed-gear-is-real-capability","t":"Placed gear is real capability"},{"id":"bays-are-concurrency","t":"Bays are concurrency"},{"id":"build-mode","t":"Build mode"},{"id":"workstations","t":"Workstations"},{"id":"truthful-telemetry","t":"Truthful telemetry"}],"s":[{"id":"placed-gear-is-real-capability","t":"Placed gear is real capability","b":"Props aren't decoration. A signal dish grants web access, a cabinet grants files, a workbench grants a terminal — and so on across the catalog. Gear is station-shared : place a thing once and your whole crew can draw on it. You don't need to duplicate props room by room. Removing a prop revokes the capability on the very next run — the floor is the permission surface, no config files."},{"id":"bays-are-concurrency","t":"Bays are concurrency","b":"Each bay you place hosts another agent running its own genuinely separate, bounded runs at the same time. A bay needs its own PC to run — and the station tells you honestly when one can't (\"NO COMPUTE — ADD A PC\") instead of pretending."},{"id":"build-mode","t":"Build mode","b":"Open build mode to lay floors, raise walls, cut doorways, and place props. The station is yours from minute one: there is no unlock tree, no gated progression, no grind. Full power immediately; the game layer is presentation, never permission."},{"id":"workstations","t":"Workstations","b":"Every agent has a home desk — chosen when the agent is created. When a run uses a tool, the agent physically walks to the workstation that grants it. That trip is driven by the real tool call in the run, which is why watching the floor tells you what's actually happening."},{"id":"truthful-telemetry","t":"Truthful telemetry","b":"The core law of the whole product: the app never asserts state the harness can't prove. No fake progress bars, no idle animations pretending to be work, no \"agents online\" counters that aren't real. If the station shows an agent at a desk mid-run, there is a live run behind it; when you see nothing happening, nothing is happening. This is what makes the window worth glancing at."}]},{"u":"troubleshooting.html","t":"Troubleshooting","g":"Troubleshooting & help","k":"Help","d":"","h":[{"id":"windows-windows-protected-your-pc","t":"Windows: \"Windows protected your PC\""},{"id":"macos-cannot-be-opened-is-damaged","t":"macOS: \"cannot be opened\" / \"is damaged\""},{"id":"updates","t":"Updates"},{"id":"uninstalling","t":"Uninstalling"},{"id":"behind-a-vpn-or-corporate-proxy","t":"Behind a VPN or corporate proxy"},{"id":"live-voice-is-slow-to-start-the-first-time","t":"Live Voice is slow to start the first time"},{"id":"still-stuck","t":"Still stuck?"}],"s":[{"id":"windows-windows-protected-your-pc","t":"Windows: \"Windows protected your PC\"","b":"The public train requires Authenticode publisher and timestamp verification, but SmartScreen can still flag a certificate while it builds reputation. Click More info , verify the publisher matches the release notes, and proceed only then. Stop if the publisher is unknown."},{"id":"macos-cannot-be-opened-is-damaged","t":"macOS: \"cannot be opened\" / \"is damaged\"","b":"A public release is required to carry a Developer ID signature and stapled Apple notarization ticket. This warning is therefore a trust failure, not an expected first-run step. Stop and report the release tag, asset name, Mac architecture, macOS version, and exact warning. Do not clear quarantine or use an unsigned-app override for a purported public release."},{"id":"updates","t":"Updates","b":"The built-in updater (System Updates) supports Windows and both Mac architectures and verifies every update against a key baked into the app. This is independent of Authenticode, Developer ID signing, and notarization. The build contract is not installed-update evidence; report a failed update and use the matching current release installer when necessary."},{"id":"uninstalling","t":"Uninstalling","b":"Windows: Settings Apps Installed apps. macOS: quit StarNet, drag it from Applications to the Trash."},{"id":"behind-a-vpn-or-corporate-proxy","t":"Behind a VPN or corporate proxy","b":"The app window honors your system proxy, but the engine's own outbound connections — AI providers and messaging channels like Telegram or Discord — go out directly and do not route through a proxy. On a network that forces one, the UI and browser sign-ins can look perfectly healthy while runs and channel connects fail with a plain network error. There is no proxy setting yet; you need direct access to the provider and platform APIs. COPY DIAGNOSTICS (under any failed turn) shows whether a proxy is configured on your machine."},{"id":"live-voice-is-slow-to-start-the-first-time","t":"Live Voice is slow to start the first time","b":"Expected, once. Hands-free Live Voice runs on a speech engine inside the app rather than a cloud service, so the first session downloads the open Whisper and Kokoro model weights and caches them in your user profile — the panel shows that download's progress instead of pretending to listen. Once cached, local listening needs no network. Speaking stays local while Kokoro is available; otherwise StarNet may use the keyless Microsoft Edge Read Aloud fallback for answer text. If the panel refuses before opening your microphone, neither the local packages nor a supported offline operating-system dictation fallback is available."},{"id":"still-stuck","t":"Still stuck?","b":"Open an issue on GitHub or email androo.agi@gmail.com . We'd rather hear about a broken thing than have you assume it's supposed to be that way."}]}];
